<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Whether this screen is big enough to be worth turning.

      False here, which is every phone: AttackFM is a one-hand app in portrait,
      and its screens - the strip, the shelves, the full-screen player - are
      built as tall columns. Turned sideways on a phone the player's artwork
      squeezes to a stamp and the shelves become two rows of nothing, so the
      rotation was never a view anyone wanted, only one they got by accident
      with the phone on its side in bed.

      The value is overridden in values-sw600dp, which is Android's own line
      for "not a phone" - 600dp of smallest width, a 7" tablet and up. A TV
      lands well past it and so keeps its landscape, which is the whole reason
      this is a resource qualifier rather than a hardcoded lock.
    -->
    <bool name="afm_rotate">false</bool>
</resources>
